---
import Project from '../../../layouts/Project.astro';
import Eyebrow from '../../../components/Eyebrow.astro';
import Chronology from '../../../components/figures/Chronology.astro';
import DirectionChart from '../../../components/figures/DirectionChart.astro';
import MechanismChart from '../../../components/figures/MechanismChart.astro';
import Residents from '../../../components/figures/Residents.astro';
import PersonDialog from '../../../components/PersonDialog.astro';
import core from '../../../data/core.json';

const c = core.counts;
const jsonld = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'Dataset',
  name: 'Buddhist Bridges: India–Korea Buddhist Mediators Dataset (V3.0)',
  description: 'Source-graded dataset of documented mediators of India–Korea Buddhist exchange, 300–2026 CE.',
  creator: { '@type': 'Person', name: 'Mohd Bilal' },
  license: 'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/',
  version: '3.0',
  datePublished: '2026-08-17',
};
---
<Project title="Buddhist Bridges — Five Bridges, One Silence"
  description="Who mediated Buddhist exchange between India and Korea, 300–2026? A source-graded prosopography of 43 documented mediators: five short-lived bridges and one five-hundred-year silence."
  citationTitle="Buddhist Bridges: Five Bridges, One Silence — India–Korea Buddhist Exchange, 300–2026"
  jsonld={jsonld}>
  <div class="wrap">
    <div class="page-head">
      <Eyebrow>Digital humanities · Korean Buddhist history</Eyebrow>
      <h1>Five bridges, one silence</h1>
      <p class="lede prose">
        Was there ever a continuous Buddhist bridge between India and Korea? The documented record
        says no. It shows five short-lived bridges, built by different kinds of people in different
        directions, and one interruption five centuries long.
      </p>
      <p class="mono muted meta-line">
        {c.people_carried} documented mediators · {c.events} events · {c.sources} graded sources ·
        dataset frozen and checksummed (V3.0, 2026) · every claim opens to its record and sources
      </p>
    </div>

    <Chronology />

    <section class="section">
      <h2><span class="mark">Part I</span>The direction inverts</h2>
      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          Before 900 the bridge was made of journeys, and it ran both ways. Of eighteen carried
          mediators, eight moved toward India and eight carried Buddhism toward Korea. No later era
          is balanced. From Goryeo onward India comes to Korea: first in one person, the monk
          Dhyānabhadra, then in texts, then in institutions. Two-way movement does not return until
          the 1990s.
        </p>
      </div>
      <DirectionChart />
      <aside class="capsule">
        <p class="label cap-kicker">The evidence, close up</p>
        <p>
          The best documented life of the balanced era is Hyecho (혜초, 704–787), who sailed to
          India around 719 and walked back through Kashmir, Persia, and Central Asia. His
          <em>Wang ocheonchukguk jeon</em>, the only surviving eighth-century East Asian account
          of India, spent a millennium in the Dunhuang cave library and was first exhibited in
          Korea in 2010. <button class="linklike" type="button" data-person="P-0001">Open his record</button>
        </p>
      </aside>
    </section>

    <section class="section">
      <h2><span class="mark">Part II</span>Five hundred years of silence</h2>
      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          The most important number in the dataset is zero. For the whole of Joseon (1392–1900) no
          person, journey, text, or institution meets the evidence bar. Eleven candidates were
          examined and all were excluded, as the Neo-Confucian state closed the channels Buddhism
          had used. Between the enshrinement of Dhyānabhadra's relics in 1370 and the next
          documented event, a delegation of 1981, lie 611 years. A narrative of unbroken
          transmission has to pass over these five centuries in silence; the record does not
          support it. What survived travelled by lineage and by print until a later generation
          could rebuild the crossing.
        </p>
        <p class="small muted">
          The zero is a finding under stated inclusion criteria, and it is documented: the eleven
          excluded candidates remain in the dataset for inspection. It is not a claim that no
          contact ever occurred.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="section">
      <h2><span class="mark">Part III</span>The bridge changes material</h2>
      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          Each rebuilding used a different substance. The ancient bridge was made of people who
          travelled. The colonial-era bridge was made of paper: four of its six mediators were not
          Buddhists, both attempted pilgrimages went unrealized, and what actually crossed was
          text, above all Tagore in Korean translation. The present bridge, built since the 1990s,
          is institutional. Orders, universities, and pilgrimage programs now carry the connection,
          movement itself has returned (seven of the eight recent mediators travelled), and the
          record includes its first women.
        </p>
      </div>
      <MechanismChart />
      <aside class="capsule">
        <p class="label cap-kicker">The evidence, close up</p>
        <p>
          Two documents anchor the colonial chapter: the six-line message Tagore sent the
          <em>Dong-a Ilbo</em> in 1929, remembered as "The Lamp of the East," and the fifteen-line
          poem later circulated under the same title, which the dataset records as a composite
          fabrication. One event closes the longer arc: in 2024, relics from Hoeamsa, where
          Dhyānabhadra's remains were enshrined in 1370, returned to Korea from the Boston Museum
          of Fine Arts. <button class="linklike" type="button" data-person="P-0030">Tagore's record</button>
        </p>
      </aside>
      <p class="prose">One role recurs in every era of contact and in no other: the mediator who stays.</p>
      <Residents />
    </section>

    <section class="section">
      <h2>Why it matters</h2>
      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          The India–Korea Buddhist relationship is usually told either as myth or as modern
          diplomacy. The documented record supports neither framing. It shows a connection built
          five times by five different cohorts, out of changing materials, and interrupted for half
          a millennium. For Korean Studies this periodizes the relationship and gives the Joseon
          interruption evidential weight. For the study of religion it shows transmission
          surviving without contact. For digital humanities it is a case for small, carefully
          graded datasets: 43 people with full sourcing can carry an argument that a large scraped
          corpus cannot.
        </p>
        <p>
          <a href="/projects/buddhist-bridges/explore/"><strong>Explore the full evidence</strong></a>
          <span class="small muted">· the complete timeline instrument and the register of all {c.people_carried} mediators</span>
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="section">
      <h2>Data and method</h2>
      <div class="method">
        <p>
          The dataset records {c.people_total} people ({c.people_carried} carried after
          Buddhist-relevance classification), {c.travels} travels, {c.texts} texts,
          {c.institutions} institutions, and {c.events} events, resting on {c.sources} graded
          sources. The rule of collection: <em>no relationship is recorded as influential
          without documentary evidence; association with both countries is not influence.</em>
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li>Every record carries a confidence grade, a completeness status, and a date-precision field. Records without a safe date are never placed on the axis.</li>
          <li>Rejected candidates stay in the dataset as documented negatives. Legendary material is labelled and excluded from analysis by default.</li>
          <li>Uncertainty is drawn, not footnoted: dashed floruit bars, hatched single-source lives, an annotated void. A 2026 survey of 186 DH projects found none that visualized uncertainty, and this project treats it as a design element.</li>
          <li>Known limits: the modern era is documented mainly through the press, Korean-language sources dominate, and no woman appears before 1990. The register records that absence; it cannot by itself explain it. All analysis is descriptive (n=43).</li>
        </ul>
        <p>
          The frozen release (13 CSVs with a SHA-256 manifest) is rebuilt deterministically from
          plain-text source modules. This page reads only a JSON export that verifies the
          manifest before every build, so the site cannot silently change the data.
        </p>
        <p class="dlrow mono">
          <a href="/data/people.json" download>people.json</a>
          <a href="/data/story.json" download>story.json</a>
          <a href="/data/timeline.json" download>timeline.json</a>
          <a href="/data/sources.json" download>sources.json</a>
          <span class="muted">CC-BY-4.0 · Zenodo DOI pending</span>
        </p>
      </div>
      <h3 id="cite">Cite</h3>
      <div class="cite">Bilal, Mohd (2026). Five Bridges, One Silence: Documented Mediators of India–Korea
Buddhist Exchange, 300–2026 (dataset V3.0). Digital humanities project. CC-BY-4.0.
DOI: pending Zenodo deposit.</div>
    </section>
  </div>

  <PersonDialog />
</Project>
<style>
  .meta-line { margin: var(--s-3) 0 0; }
  .method { background: var(--surface); padding: var(--s-3) var(--s-4); max-width: var(--w-wide); font-size: var(--t-small); }
  .method p:last-child { margin-bottom: 0; }
  .method ul { padding-left: 1.2rem; }
  .dlrow { display: flex; flex-wrap: wrap; align-items: center; gap: var(--s-1); margin: var(--s-2) 0 0; }
  .dlrow a { display: inline-flex; align-items: center; min-height: 40px; padding: 0 0.7rem; border: 1px solid var(--hairline); border-radius: var(--radius); text-decoration: none; color: var(--ink); background: var(--paper); transition: border-color var(--ease), color var(--ease); }
  .dlrow a:hover { border-color: var(--accent); color: var(--accent); }
  .dlrow .muted { margin-left: var(--s-1); }
  @media (max-width: 639px) { .method { padding: var(--s-2) var(--s-3); } }
</style>
